Hello Walter, thanks for sharing this, an interesting one\! Please see my advice in purple below. 

Are we allowed to mention Splend in our ads or just mention we have "vouchers" with a partnered company? {color:#6554c0}*This would depend on your agreement with Splend i.e. whether you can use their brand name in what is essentially a Bolt advert. You should get some form of licence from Splend that specifies how Splend will be referred to in the giveaway. Bolt might have a licence already based on any previous Bolt/Splend relationship.* {color}


Do we need to disclaim about Splend's additional checks or that is between the driver and Splend? {color:#6554c0}*Would it be a condition of entry that the drivers meet certain insurance and credit check requirements? I don’t think Bolt can run a giveaway, announce the winner, pass their details onto Splend and then Splend let them know they have failed the checks. Key point for giveaways is that we need to give all conditions of entry at the outset.*{color}

{color:#6554c0}*Usually giveaways involving another company would say something like “*{color}{color:#6554c0}*_in partnership with/sponsored by_*{color}{color:#6554c0}*” a third party brand e.g. Splend. I assume it will be Bolt managing the whole process, we just have permission to use Splend’s branding. I imagine we would just say something in the giveaway terms like - at the end of the initial 2 months you will automatically be enrolled in a \[INSERT] month contract with Splend which will be subject to Splend’s terms and conditions found* {color}{color:#6554c0}*_here_*{color}{color:#6554c0}*.* {color}

{color:#6554c0}*Also, we will need to include a notice on how the driver’s personal data will be shared with Splend (and any other parties).*{color}

I hope this is all helpful. Let me know wants this has been mapped out further if you need terms.

Thanks,

Rebecca

@Dan, I ran the wording of the insurance part by my father who is in the insurance business and it is standard wording and conditions for a business lease. We are only to insure glass and sanitary, e.g. that if we break windows, the toilet or sink, we pay for it ourselves. The other insurance premiums are completely up to ourselves, like how much do we want to insure our things stored in the warehouse for, so a general contents insurance would suffice with an amount we decide with the insurance company.

“_If, due to the tenant's use of the leased premises, the property's insurance premiums are increased or expenses are imposed on the property, the tenant shall reimburse the landlord herefor._” This should not apply to us. This would be e.g. a store that sold fireworks or something that has a high risk for the landlord.



@Phillip, you are right on the we break, we pay, but other than that we just need to keep the place in order and have it in the same condition the day we end our lease, painting, cleaning etc.

