It is important to understand why something was created and the difference between them {quote}

This is my end goal, understanding why there are three roles for sandboxes and what each one does, what the differences are, etc. It is not always obvious when there is no historical context or written documentation. 

{quote}Question for Leandro please explain wahts the difference between {{/mysql-ansible/roles/mysql-sandbox}} & {{./mysql-server-tools/roles/create-sandbox}}{quote}

I am not entirely sure. It looks like:

{{/mysql-ansible/roles/mysql-sandbox}} → takes ec2 instance and adds: extra network interface, EBS volume for LXD images, installs and init ZFS + LXD 

{{./mysql-server-tools/roles/create-sandbox}} → takes an ec2 instance (already with extra NIC+LXD+ZFS) and creates the LXD containers/profiles+my.cnf

OTOH, {{./mysql-sandbox-recreate}} seems to include code for some of the tasks above (eg: copy my.cnf, add extra NICs, etc) and even clones {{/mysql-server-tools}}. So maybe this role somehow refactored some of the previous code from the two mentioned above? 

It’s obvious I have gaps in my knowledge and am trying to find out the answers, but reverse engineering the whole code base takes time and is not easy. The less irrelevant code there is the more manageable this task is. 


{quote}And second question please explain how removing any of this helps to fight the growth and load on running servers ?

Sorry, but I think cleanup is pointless …{quote}

The goal of cleaning up is not to fight load on servers but rather to remove irrelevant code that is no longer used. 
Without this extra legacy bloat, it is easier to decide which code is worth studying and which is just deprecated time wasting. IMHO, it’s better to clean up as you go along rather then wait years and years and then running into a huge and daunting clean up task.

