_6. What do you think the risks are to those subject to live tracing? If this data and the live tracing was, for example, accessed without authorisation, stolen, leaked or destroyed, what harms might come to the individuals?_
{quote}

This is mostly around case 1 and case 3 where individuals(customers) are involved directly.

If data is destroyed - no harm.

As we aim for all data, for "bad access" I guess any sort of harm you could think of accessing any data passing between backend and frontend.

For case 2, anonymous data. There is no harm for individuals at all. There is harm for an org as it will expose internals.

{quote}
_7. Will rights access management protocols remain unchanged? That's to say will anybody ordinarily not privy, or without routine access to this personal data, be granted access and insight?_{quote}

For cases 1 and 3, I guess we have to design some sort of access management process here, as the data is pretty sensitive.

For case 2, I think we don't need much access control here.

{quote}

_8. Has the expert Head of IT Security - i.e. Tarmo - been consulted, and has be raised any concerns which cannot be mitigated?_
{quote}

 Yes. Tarmo Randel was involved into this as well.

His concerns are about a) PCI data b) Secrets/API Keys, etc

One way or another end-solution will have to mitigate these, otherwise these are significant blockers for a project.

{quote}
_9. Can the trace data be anonymised at any point whatsoever? E.g. Can a written analysis of the findings be authored which omits the personal data at (2)? On conclusion of the project, how long do you intend to keep the raw data i.e. the individual tracing results attached to the identifiable user data._{quote}

# Anonymisation is possible in general. 

# For cases 1 and 3 what could be the scenario? Internal/External user complains about some behavior. They give/Already gave consent for data to be traced. Mobile/Backend developer analysis traces and as a result he should prepare a bug report/bugfix task, when he should specify details as sufficient as to help resolve the initial case. Which most likely means, that in some way some sort of personal data might occur in a report.

# We should agree on a timeline. We could make it as small as 1week and might be as big as 3 months. Afterwards we either delete it, or send to some sort of encrypted storage, which no one can access.

{quote}

_10. I'm already satisfied that there's a strong legitimate interest in this case and I would, ordinarily, about now be undertaking a balancing exercise to determine whether Bolt's interests are overridden by other's interest and whether additional safeguards and mitigations are appropriate. But this is industrial research and you mentioned you would look to voluntary participation and consent from subjects. Is this still the case?_
{quote}

For cases 1 and 3, where data and especially PII data is involved, we should by default assume consent only solution. Might be even some time limited. E.g. I am able to provide consent for 1 month period or so..

For case 2, where no data is involved, only telemetry I am not sure we need any consent, nor any sort of EULA changes. 

{quote}
